Ladybird places the “top”, “middle”, and “bottom” canvas text baselines
on an em square as tall as the font size, divided at the alphabetic
baseline in the proportion of the font’s ascent and descent. Gecko does
the same: gfxFont::CalculateDerivedMetrics() derives emAscent and
emDescent that way, and CanvasRenderingContext2D uses them for those
three baselines. Blink scales the OS/2 typo ascent and descent to the
font size instead, in SimpleFontData::NormalizedTypoAscentAndDescent(),
which TextMetrics::GetFontBaseline() uses. And WebKit places them on an
em square too, one that emHeightAscentAndDescent() supplies to its
CanvasRenderingContext2DBase::textOffset(). For bold 80px Arial, the
alphabetic baseline is now 64.8px below “top” in Ladybird, 64.9px in
Firefox, and 62.1px in Chrome.
